        "corpus_chunk": 9,
        "section": "results",
        "text": "ularly PFOS [ 70 , 71 ], with some evidence that sorption may affect separation by RO [ 70 ]. However, many of these studies have been performed with flat sheet coupons or using isotherms, which may not represent sorption during full-scale treatment. PFAS sorption in mem - brane systems is not limited to the membrane itself [ 41 ], and PFAS sorption to treatment materials is not unique to membrane systems [ 72 \u2013 75 ]. To better understand PFAS residuals emanating from treat - ment processes, including pretreatment, PFAS sorption throughout treatment processes needs to be considered. This paper aims to evaluate and compare the performance of RO and NF membranes for PFAS treatment using data generated during a pilot demonstration. A CCMS was operated at 80 %, 85 %, and 90 % system recoveries for treatment of an AFFF-impacted groundwater with two different commercially available membranes (brackish water RO and tight NF). The operational performance of both membranes was compared through net driving pressure (NDP), temperature-corrected specific flux (TCSF), and energy use. High concentrations of multiple PFAS in the treated groundwater allowed for a more precise measure - ment of rejection by NF and RO. Rejection of multiple classes of PFAS were compared between both membranes at the three different re - coveries."
      },
      {
