        "section": "abstract",
        "text": "weights ( > 400 g/mole) of these PFAS. NF has been shown to be an effective treatment approach for fluorotelomer-impacted waters where drinking water treatments including activated carbon were unable to removal all PFAS [ 31 ]. The low NPDWR for PFOS and PFOA in drinking water (4 ng/L [ 14 ]), coupled with the elevated concentrations of PFAS and co-contaminants at AFFF-impacted sites make RO and NF treatment a more effective option. NF membranes have received more interest for PFAS treatment applications due to lower pressure and energy re - quirements compared to RO [ 32 ] and partial passage of ions and organic matter [ 33 ]. However, several publications have highlighted that PFAS rejection by RO and NF, particularly loose NF, is impacted by ionic strength and composition [ 34 \u2013 39 ], pH [ 39 , 40 ], PFAS chain length [ 39 \u2013 42 ], and feed water concentration [ 39 , 40 , 43 ]. Safulko et al. [ 44 ] compared loose NF, tight NF, brackish water RO, and saltwater RO membranes at the pilot scale and reported that except for loose NF, all evaluated membranes achieved similar and effective PFAA rejection ( > 98 %) even at high system recovery (up to 97 %). These results"
