        "section": "results",
        "text": "revealed that they may be more toxic than the more well studied PFAAs [ 52 \u2013 55 ]. Reported pKa \u2019 s for FASAs (ranging from 3.3 to 6.5 [ 40 , 56 \u2013 58 ]) indicate that their rejection may be more impacted by pH and reduced compared to anionic PFAAs and fluorotelomer sulfonates (FTSs) [ 40 , 44 ]. There is a need to assess the pilot- and full-scale treatment performance of RO and NF with real water matrices for PFAAs and beyond. A major barrier to widespread implementation of high-pressure membrane filtration is the production and management of the concen - trated waste stream (retentate). However, concentration by membranes can potentially be used to improve the prospects for PFAS destruction, which is costly and difficult to implement at large scales [ 29 , 59 ]. Simultaneous treatment and concentration of PFAS impacted waters using membrane technologies has been evaluated in several recent studies, with the goal of more effective PFAS destruction [ 42 , 49 , 59 \u2013 62 ]. However, traditional multistage high-pressure membrane systems are limited to ~85 % recovery [ 63 ] and are not well optimized for solute separation and energy utilization [ 29 , 64 , 65 ]. To mitigate retentate disposal challenges and optimize energy efficiency and solute separa -"
