        "corpus_chunk": 8,
        "section": "results",
        "text": "To mitigate retentate disposal challenges and optimize energy efficiency and solute separa - tion, several high recovery membrane configurations have been devel - oped [ 66 ]. One such configuration, closed-circuit membrane systems (CCMS), recycles retentate to decouple flux, feed flow rate, and recov - ery, resulting in optimized energy use, separation efficiency, facility footprint, and system recovery [ 67 ]. CCMS have been shown to have greater energy efficiency and removal performance compared to tradi - tional multistage systems due to consistent permeate flux across the system [ 33 ]. The negative impact of high recovery operation on permeate quality while using CCMS have been demonstrated on various water types in several recent studies [ 44 , 63 , 68 , 69 ], indicating that while high recoveries can be reliably attained, target permeate con - centrations are an important consideration when planning system recovery. A major concern for any PFAS treatment technology is disposal re - quirements for PFAS-containing treatment materials [ 23 ] and literature on the sorption of PFAS to treatment materials such as filters, non- adsorbent resins, and membranes is limited. Several papers have indi - cated that PFAS sorbs to membranes during treatment [ 40 , 41 ], partic - ularly PFOS [ 70 , 71 ], with some evidence that sorption may affect separation by RO [ 70 ]."
